9@P1: alignment of particles transported by the fluid [32,33],\nL170@P1: providing valuable insight into the stretching and rotation\nL171@P1: of fluid elements over time and space [34]. FTLEs allow us\nL172@P1: to identify Lagrangian coherent structures [20–22]; fluid\u0002velocity structures that help to organize and understand\nL173@P1: complex spatiotemporal flow patterns [35]. These geomet\u0002rical structures appear as ridges of large maximal FTLEs,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://purehost.bath.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/205056987/OddElasticity_MainText.pdf)\nciteturn19view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn18view7\",\"pattern\":\"work\"}); Total lines: 1183\nL19@P0: Take down policy\nL20@P0: If you believe that this document breaches copyright please contact us providing details, and we will remove access to the work immediately\nL21@P0: and investigate your claim.\nL22@P0-1: Download date: 15. Oct. 2026Odd elasticity\nL23@P1: Colin Scheibner,1, 2, ∗ Anton Souslov,1, 3, ∗ Debarghya Banerjee,4, 5\nL24@P1: Piotr Sur´owka,6 William T. M. Irvine,1, 2, 7 and Vincenzo Vitelli1, 2, †\nL25@P1: 1\nL26@P1: James Franck Institute, The University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois 60637, USA\nL27@P1: 2Department of Physics, The University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois 60637, USA\nL28@P1: 3Department of Physics, University of Bath, Bath BA2 7AY, United Kingdom\nL29@P1: 4Max Planck Institute for Dynamics and Self-Organization, 37077 G¨ottingen, Germany\nL30@P1: 5\nL31@P1: Instituut-Lorentz, Universiteit Leiden, Leiden 2300 RA, The Netherlands\nL32@P1: 6Max Planck Institute for the Physics of Complex Systems, 01187 Dresden, Germany\nL33@P1: 7Enrico Fermi Institute, The University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, 60637, USA\nL34@P1: A passive solid cannot do work on its surround\u0002ings through any quasistatic cycle of deforma\u0002tions [1, 2]. This constraint need not hold in\nL35@P1: active solids, to which energy is supplied by in\u0002ternal batteries, external fields, or biochemical\nL36@P1: sources [3–11]. Previous studies have elucidated\nL37@P1: how activity can dramatically modify the values\nL38@P1: of the elastic moduli [12–14] already present in\nL39@P1: passive elasticity. In this Letter, we show that\nL40@P1: static elastic moduli altogether absent in passive\nL41@P1: elasticity can arise from active, nonconservative\nL42@P1: microscopic interactions. These active moduli en\u0002ter the antisymmetric (or odd) part of the static\nL43@P1: elastic modulus tensor and quantify the amount of\nL44@P1: work extracted along quasistatic strain cycles. In\nL45@P1: 2D isotropic media, two chiral odd-elastic moduli\nL46@P1: emerge in addition to the bulk and shear moduli.\nL47@P1: We discuss microscopic realizations that include\nL48@P1: networks of Hookean springs augmented with ac\u0002tive transverse forces and non-reciprocal active\nL49@P1: hinges. Using coarse-grained microscopic mod\u0002els, numerical simulations, and continuum equa\u0002tions, we uncover phenomena ranging from aux\u0002etic behavior [14–18] induced by odd moduli to\nL50@P1: elastic wave propagation in overdamped media\nL51@P1: enabled by self-sustained active strain cycles. Our\nL52@P1: work sheds light on the non-Hermitian mechanics\nL53@P1: of two- and three-dimensional active solids that\nL54@P1: conserve linear momentum but exhibit a non\u0002reciprocal [4, 19] linear response.\nL55@P1: We begin with a simple microscopic view of odd elastic\u0002ity. Fig. 1a shows a concrete illustration of an odd elastic\nL56@P1: solid as a network of masses connected by active bonds.\nL57@P1: Each bond contains two battery-powered propellers that\nL58@P1: blow air at a constant rate. When the spring inside the\nL59@P1: bond elongates (contracts), a gear system rotates the pro\u0002pellers to produce transverse forces proportional to the\nL60@P1: strain, see Supplementary Movie 1. For small strains, the\nL61@P1: force law is given by\nL62@P1: F(r) = (−kˆr + k\nL63@P1: aφˆ) δr, (1)\nL64@P1: ∗ These authors contributed equally to this work.\nL65@P1: † vitelli@uchicago.edu\nL66@P1: FIG. 1. Quasistatic cycles with nonconservative in\u0002teractions. a. A mechanical realization of Eq. (1). Two pro\u0002pellers, mounted on platforms connected by a Hookean spring,\nL67@P1: are powered by batteries and blow air at a constant rate. As\nL68@P1: the platforms slide together or apart, a gear system rotates\nL69@P1: the propellers, giving rise to transverse forces. An elongated\nL70@P1: configuration is shown. A triangular lattice built out of such\nL71@P1: active bonds exhibits odd elasticity. b-c. When linearized, the\nL72@P1: bond gives rise to Eq. (1), which features an active transverse\nL73@P1: force (red arrows) proportional to strain (black arrows). (The\nL74@P1: Hookean spring provides a radial restoring force not shown.)\nL75@P1: This interaction is non-reciprocal: extension and compression\nL76@P1: induce torque, while rotation does not induce or relieve ten\u0002sion. Nonetheless, the interaction conserves linear momentum\nL77@P1: because the forces on each end of the bond are equal and op\u0002posite. d-e. When the bond is brought on a strain-controlled\nL78@P1: quasistatic cycle, the work done by the radial forces Fr on leg\nL79@P1: \nL80@P1: 1 -\nL81@P1: 2 and leg\nL82@P1: 3 -\nL83@P1: 4 sums to zero. However, the transverse\nL84@P1: force Fφ does work on leg\nL85@P1: 2 -\nL86@P1: 3 that is not compensated for\nL87@P1: elsewhere on the cycle. For small angles δφ and strains δr/r0,\nL88@P1: the work done by the bond on a quasistatic cycle is equal to\nL89@P1: k\nL90@P1: a\nL91@P1: times the area enclosed.\nL92@P1-2: where δr = r − r0 is the radial displacement from the2\nL93@P2: equilibrium length, r0, and ˆr and φˆ are the unit vec\u0002tors parallel and perpendicular to the bond, respectively.\nL94@P2: Eq. (1) describes a Hookean spring of spring constant k\nL95@P2: with an additional chiral, transverse force proportional\nL96@P2: to k\nL97@P2: a\nL98@P2: , see Fig. 1b-c. At first sight, Eq. (1) seems merely\nL99@P2: to be a transverse generalization of Hooke’s law. How\u0002ever, the non-vanishing ∇ × F = k\nL100@P2: a\nL101@P2: implies that this\nL102@P2: pairwise force law is necessarily active. For small dis\u0002placements, any (quasistatic) closed path will extract or\nL103@P2: inject a non-zero amount of energy or work W =\nL104@P2: H\nL105@P2: F · dl\nL106@P2: equal (by Green’s theorem) to k\nL107@P2: a\nL108@P2: times the area enclosed,\nL109@P2: see Fig. 1d-e. While the example in Fig. 1 experiences a\nL110@P2: net torque in response to elongation or compression, this\nL111@P2: need not be the case if the active forces are non-pairwise.\nL112@P2: In the S.I., we present such a system using active hinges\nL113@P2: that do not require sources of angular momentum, yet\nL114@P2: still exhibit quasistatic path-dependent work. In both\nL115@P2: our microscopic models, the interactions share three cru\u0002cial features: (i) they conserve linear momentum, (ii)\nL116@P2: they depend only on the relative positions of the par\u0002ticles, and (iii) they do not follow from a potential en\u0002ergy [12]. More realistic implementations of these active\nL117@P2: bonds and hinges may use piezoelectric components [20],\nL118@P2: robotic tentacles [21, 22], or mechanochemical interac\u0002tions [23].\nL119@P2: The microscopic units in Fig. 1, when connected in a\nL120@P2: network, violate one of the fundamental assumptions of\nL121@P2: classical elasticity: the existence of an elastic potential\nL122@P2: energy. Since the energetic state of each microscopic unit\nL123@P2: has quasistatic path dependence, the notion of an elas\u0002tic potential energy is not well defined. Nonetheless, a\nL124@P2: stress-strain relation exists and can be linearized for small\nL125@P2: deformations. This approximation, known as Hooke’s\nL126@P2: law, is captured by the equation σij = Cijmnumn, where\nL127@P2: umn are the gradients ∂mun of the displacement vector\nL128@P2: un, and Cijmn is the elastic modulus tensor. In the ab\u0002sence of an elastic potential energy, the most general lin\u0002ear relationship between stress and displacement gradient\nL129@P2: for a two-dimensional isotropic solid reads:\nL130@P2: , (2)\nL131@P2: provided that no coupling to rotations is allowed, see\nL132@P2: Methods.\nL133@P2: The notation in Eq. (2) is a geometric representation\nL134@P2: of Hooke’s law σij = Cijmnumn. The displacement gra\u0002dients on the right-hand side are decomposed along four\nL135@P2: independent components: dilation, rotation, and the two\nL136@P2: shear deformations S1 and S2, i.e. irreducible represen\u0002tations of SO(2). Similarly, the stress vector on the left\u0002hand side is decomposed into pressure, torque, and the\nL137@P2: two shear stresses. Unlike passive isotropic media, the\nL138@P2: solid represented in Eq. (2) has four independent elastic\nL139@P2: moduli, not two. Besides the familiar bulk modulus, B,\nL140@P2: and shear modulus, µ, there are additional entries A and\nL141@P2: FIG. 2. Odd elastic engine cycle. a. The odd modulus\nL142@P2: A couples compression to an internal torque density, while\nL143@P2: rotations induce no stresses. The applied strains are repre\u0002sented by the black arrows, the undeformed shape by the dot\u0002ted lines, and the internal stresses by the blue icons. b. The\nL144@P2: odd modulus Kocouples the two independent shear deforma\u0002tions. Unlike shear coupling in anisotropic passive solids, the\nL145@P2: induced stress is always rotated 45◦counterclockwise relative\nL146@P2: to the applied strain. c. An odd elastic material is subjected\nL147@P2: to a closed cycle in deformation space. First, a counterclock\u0002wise rotation is followed by a volumetric strain \u000fV , inducing\nL148@P2: a torque density A\u000fV . Next, the object does work A\u000fV \u000fθ on\nL149@P2: its surrounding as it is rotated clockwise through an angle \u000fθ,\nL150@P2: before being compressed to its original size. The total work\nL151@P2: done is A times the area enclosed in deformation space: \u000fV \u000fθ.\nL152@P2: d. An analogous cycle involving only shear stress and shear\nL153@P2: strain.\nL154@P2: Ko. Qualitatively, the modulus A couples compression\nL155@P2: (and dilation) to an internal torque density, see Fig. 2a.\nL156@P2: By contrast, Ko does not entail a net torque density, but\nL157@P2: rather implies a 45◦rotation between the applied shear\nL158@P2: strain and the resulting shear stress, see Fig. 2b. The\nL159@P2: asymmetry of the elastic modulus matrix in Eq. (2) im\u0002plies a non-reciprocal, linear mechanical response, echo\u0002ing the non-reciprocal linear response of a single bond\nL160@P2: in Fig. 1. In terms of microscopic parameters, we find\nL161@P2: B = 2µ =\nL162@P2: √\nL163@P2: 3\nL164@P2: 2\nL165@P2: k and A = 2Ko =\nL166@P2: √\nL167@P2: 3\nL168@P2: 2\nL169@P2: k\nL170@P2: a\nL171@P2: for a triangular\nL172@P2: lattice. As shown in the S.I., a honeycomb lattice with\nL173@P2: next-nearest-neighbor springs allows all four moduli to be\nL174@P2: tuned independently. Furthermore, we show that A = 0\nL175@P2: identically for models based on active hinges rather than\nL176@P2: active springs, since the hinges do not require microscopic\nL177@P2: torques, see S.I.\nL178@P2: The moduli A and Ko violate the symmetry of the elas\u0002tic modulus tensor Cijmn = Cmnij , which applies when\u0002ever the stresses arise from gradients of a free energy\nL179@P2: f =\nL180@P2: 1\nL181@P2: 2Cijmnuijumn. Microscopic units with quasistatic\nL182@P2: path-dependent work, e.g. those in Fig. 1, can give rise\nL183@P2: to an additional contribution to the elastic modulus ten\u0002sor: Cijmn = C\nL184@P2: e\nL185@P2: ijmn + C\nL186@P2: o\nL187@P2: ijmn with C\nL188@P2: o\nL189@P2: ijmn = −C\nL190@P2: o\nL191@P2-3: mnij ,3\nL192@P3: which we refer to as odd elasticity since it is antisymmet\u0002ric (or odd) under exchange of the first and second pair\nL193@P3: of indices. For example, Ko and A are the additional\nL194@P3: moduli that arise when specialized to two-dimensional\nL195@P3: isotropic media. Such moduli are forbidden by energy\nL196@P3: conservation, but allowed in active media and metama\u0002terials with nonconservative interactions. For example,\nL197@P3: the modulus Kois compatible with broken microscopic\nL198@P3: time-reversal symmetry in active biological surfaces [24].\nL199@P3: Odd elasticity is distinct from activity-induced negative\nL200@P3: compressibility [13, 14], which pertains only to the sym\u0002metric part of Cijmn, and from viscous effects [2], which\nL201@P3: vanish in the quasistatic limit.\nL202@P3: Both A and Ko are chiral, i.e. they describe a solid\nL203@P3: in which the action of a parity inversion is not equiva\u0002lent to a rotation. Their chiralities are set by the sign\nL204@P3: of the induced torque and the direction of rotation be\u0002tween shear strain and shear stress, respectively. Here,\nL205@P3: chirality arises from activity, i.e. from the non-potential\nL206@P3: interactions, not from additional microscopic degrees of\nL207@P3: freedom, cf. passive Cosserat elasticity [25–27]. In the\nL208@P3: S.I., we show that anisotropic 2D odd elastic solids need\nL209@P3: not be chiral, and the elastic modulus tensor in 3D is\nL210@P3: always achiral irrespective of odd elasticity. A full classi\u0002fication of odd elasticity in 3D is obtained by decompos\u0002ing the strain tensor using irreducible representations of\nL211@P3: SO(3), see S.I. A 3D odd elastic solid must necessarily\nL212@P3: be anisotropic and the elastic modulus tensor displays up\nL213@P3: to 36 additional moduli that cannot be derived from an\nL214@P3: elastic potential. Odd elasticity cannot exist in 1D, since\nL215@P3: the elastic modulus tensor is a scalar, which cannot be\nL216@P3: antisymmetric.\nL217@P3: Since C\nL218@P3: o\nL219@P3: ijmn cannot be obtained from a free energy,\nL220@P3: an odd elastic solid may be taken through a closed cy\u0002cle of quasistatic deformations with non-zero total work\nL221@P3: ∆w =\nL222@P3: H\nL223@P3: C\nL224@P3: o\nL225@P3: ijmnumnduij done by (or on) the material, as\nL226@P3: anticipated by the microscopic cycle shown in Fig. 1d-e.\nL227@P3: (See S.I. for a proof.) In Fig. 2c, we apply this general for\u0002mula to a two-dimensional isotropic solid and illustrate\nL228@P3: such a cycle using rotations and dilations. The initial\nL229@P3: and final configurations are identical, hence, zero work is\nL230@P3: done by the conservative part C\nL231@P3: e\nL232@P3: ijmn. By contrast, the to\u0002tal work done due to the odd contribution C\nL233@P3: o\nL234@P3: ijmn is equal\nL235@P3: to the modulus A times the area enclosed by the cycle\nL236@P3: in the space of rotations and dilations. Fig. 2d shows\nL237@P3: an analogous cycle which involves only shear stress and\nL238@P3: shear strain. In 3D there are four independent cycles,\nL239@P3: see S.I. During an odd-elastic cycle, the energy gener\u0002ated or lost depends only on the geometry of the path\nL240@P3: through strain space and not on the strain rate u˙ mn, in\nL241@P3: contrast to friction or dissipation, which always lead to\nL242@P3: energy loss. Other active solids like muscles do work by\nL243@P3: a different mechanism [28]: as they elongate and con\u0002tract along the same path in strain space, their active\nL244@P3: stresses change according to chemical signals instead of\nL245@P3: strain. By contrast, stresses due to odd elasticity depend\nL246@P3: on strain alone, and the work extracted depends on the\nL247@P3: area enclosed in strain space.\nL248@P3: To illustrate how odd elasticity compares to other man\u0002ifestations of activity in solids, we write the linear active\nL249@P3: forces F\nL250@P3: a\nL251@P3: j\nL252@P3: in the following more general form (see Meth\u0002ods):\nL253@P3: F\nL254@P3: a\nL255@P3: j = gj (u, u˙ , ∇u, . . .) + ∂i\nL256@P3: \nL257@P3: Cijmnumn + ηijmnu˙ mn\u0001\nL258@P3: .\nL259@P3: (3)\nL260@P3: The first term gj summarizes non-viscoelastic active\nL261@P3: forces. These forces can be constant or explicitly pro\u0002portional to displacement ui\nL262@P3: , velocity ˙ui, strain uij [4,\nL263@P3: 29, 30], strain rate ˙uij , or to fields other than ui, such\nL264@P3: as temperature and electromagnetic fields [31, 32], or the\nL265@P3: nematic order parameter [5]. This term includes active\nL266@P3: body forces such as those exhibited by solids formed by\nL267@P3: self-propelled particles that manifestly violate conserva\u0002tion of linear momentum [33, 34]. The second term on the\nL268@P3: right-hand side of Eq. (3) captures the forces that result\nL269@P3: from the divergence of the viscoelastic stress tensor, i.e.,\nL270@P3: from two spatial derivatives of displacement and velocity.\nL271@P3: It is well known that energy sources can renormalize the\nL272@P3: values of the passive elastic moduli or viscous coefficients\nL273@P3: that enter the symmetric part of Cijmn or ηijmn (e.g.\nL274@P3: negative compressibility [13, 14] and eddy viscosity [35]).\nL275@P3: Activity can also result in odd (or Hall) viscosity, which\nL276@P3: is the antisymmetric part of the viscosity tensor denoted\nL277@P3: by η\nL278@P3: o\nL279@P3: ijmn = −η\nL280@P3: o\nL281@P3: mnij [36–40]. However, all the aforemen\u0002tioned effects are physically distinct from odd elasticity,\nL282@P3: which pertains to the antisymmetric part of Cijmn and\nL283@P3: is a crucial, but previously absent, piece in presenting\nL284@P3: a unified picture of the phenomenology of linear active\nL285@P3: solids.\nL286@P3: In the presence of odd elasticity, even the most famil\u0002iar elastic phenomena appear in a new guise. Consider\nL377@P4: of an autonomous, self-sustaining elastic engine cycle, in\nL378@P4: which the system converts internal energy into mechan\u0002ical work to offset dissipative losses [cf. Fig. 2c]. The\nL519@P6: along the line ΓK and the bands open. The middle in\u0002set of Fig. 5b highlights the regions in the Brillouin zone\nL520@P6: (light grey) for which waves can propagate when, as an\nL521@P6: example,\nL522@P6: \nL523@P6: \nL524@P6: \nL525@P6: \nL526@P6: k\nL527@P6: a\nL528@P6: k\nL529@P6: \nL530@P6: \nL531@P6: \nL532@P6: \nL533@P6: is given by the horizontal dashed line. The\nL534@P6: surprising feature is the existence of waves at short length\nL535@P6: scales well below the critical value in the continuum the\u0002ory of Fig. 5a.\nL536@P6: In summary, our work brings to light a neglected facet\nL537@P6: of linear elasticity in systems equipped with an internal\nL538@P6: source of energy. Future work will explore applications of\nL539@P6: odd elasticity to biomechanical systems [11, 24, 43, 44],\nL540@P6: kinematics of systems with transverse interactions such\nL541@P6: as gyroscopes or vortex lattices [45], viscoelastic quantum\nL542@P6: Hall states [46] and active metamaterials [4, 47] function\u0002ing as emergent soft robots that harvest energy, transmit\nL543@P6: it using odd mechanical waves, and perform work at des\u0002ignated sites. In addition, odd elasticity provides an al\u0002ternative approach to design energy-absorbing materials\nL544@P6: that exploit quasistatic cycles instead of rate-dependent\nL545@P6: deformations.\nL546@P6: Acknowledgments A.S., W.T.M.I., and V.V. ac\u0002knowledge primary support through the Chicago MR\u0002SEC, funded by the NSF through grant DMR-1420709.\nL547@P6: C.S. was supported by the National Science Founda\u0002tion Graduate Research Fellowship under Grant No.\nL548@P6: 1746045. W.T.M.I. acknowledges support from NSF\nL549@P6: EFRI NewLAW grant 1741685 and NSF DMR 1905974.\nL550@P6: P.S. was supported by the Deutsche Forschungsgemein\u0002schaft via the Leibniz Program. We thank F. J¨ulicher\nL551@P6: and G. Salbreux for a critical reading of the manuscript.\nL552@P6: I. METHODS\nL553@P6: A. Elastic energy and symmetries of the elastic\nL554@P6: modulus tensor\nL555@P6: The standard theory of elasticity begins with the pos\u0002tulation of an elastic free energy density f (see e.g.,\nL556@P6: Ref. [1]). The free energy density is a function of the\nL853@P8: 1\nL854@P8: 2\nL855@P8: (ΛikΛkj − δij ).\nL856@P8: The full tensor u\nL857@P8: nl\nL858@P8: ij is rotationally invariant at all\nL859@P8: orders, and at linear order reduces to u\nL860@P8: s\nL861@P8: ij . If Cijmn\nL862@P8: has the minor symmetry Cijmn = Cijnm, then\nL863@P8: the product Cijmnumn is the same whether or not\nL864@P8: umn is symmetrized. We choose to work with\nL865@P8: the displacement gradient tensor umn (i.e., unsym\u0002metrized strain) to be explicit about the assump\u0002tion of non-coupling to rotation. Under DD alone,\nL866@P8: the elastic modulus tensor contains 12 independent\nL867@P8: moduli.\nL943@P8: 0 0 0 µ\nL944@P8: \nL945@P8: . (24)\nL946@P8: Here, B is the familiar bulk modulus, which is the pro\u0002portionality constant between compression and pressure;\nL947@P8-9: µ is the shear modulus, which is the proportionality con\u0002stant between shear stress and shear strain.9\nL948@P9: In this work, we retain only deformation dependence\nL949@P9: and isotropy. We assume deformation dependence be\u0002cause stress only arises in the solids we consider as a\nL950@P9: result of relative displacements (i.e., changes in the ma\u0002terial’s metric). Note that isotropy is not a strict require\u0002ment, and many crystalline solids have anisotropic elastic\nL951@P9: modulus tensors. However, we consider only the isotropic\nL952@P9: case for simplicity. In this work we study odd elasticity,\nL953@P9: which arises when we lift the assumption of an elastic\nL954@P9: potential energy (i.e., conservation of energy). Assum\u0002ing only isotropy and deformation dependence, the most\nL955@P9: general form of the elastic modulus tensor is\nL1065@P10: many of the ways in which activity can manifest in con\u0002tinuum theories of solids. The term gj includes the forces\nL1066@P10: F\nL1067@P10: χ\nL1068@P10: i\nL1069@P10: and the first four terms of Eq. (29). The second term\nL1070@P10: in Eq. (3) highlights explicitly the role of viscoelasticity.\nL1071@P10: Our contribution in this work is to highlight that activ\u0002ity can introduce an antisymmetric part of the elastic\nL1072@P10: modulus tensor and to explore its phenomenology and\nL1073@P10: microscopic origins.\n"
